.\" Manual page for slipcase-desktop.
.\"
.\" It exists because the binary takes an argument and has no --help to
.\" describe it: src/main.rs reads args_os().nth(1) and nothing else, so this
.\" is the only place that argument is written down. lintian tagged its
.\" absence and CHECKLIST.md carries the decision to write it rather than
.\" override the tag.
.\"
.\" @VERSION@ is substituted by packaging/debian/build-deb.sh, the same way
.\" control.in is, so the version in the header cannot drift from the package.
.\" The date is the date this page was last revised and not the build date,
.\" which is the convention and is also what makes the page reproducible.
.\"
.\" Author: David M. Anderson
.\" Built with AI assistance (Claude, Anthropic)
.TH SLIPCASE\-DESKTOP 1 "2026\-08\-26" "slipcase\-desktop @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
slipcase\-desktop \- viewer and metadata editor for slipcase containers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B slipcase\-desktop
.RI [ container ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Slipcase
opens a slipcase container, shows its metadata as an editable tree, and hands
the payload to whatever application the desktop has registered for it.
Containers are read and written entirely through the slpc library; this
application parses none of its own.
.PP
It is a windowed application, normally started by opening a container from a
file manager rather than from a shell. The package registers the media type,
the desktop entry, and the icons that make that work, so a container opens on
a double\-click.
.SH OPTIONS
There are none.
.B slipcase\-desktop
is a window rather than a command\-line interface, and it takes one optional
positional argument and nothing else. For a command\-line interface to the
container format, see
.BR slipcase (1).
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I container
The container to open. With no argument the window opens empty and offers to
choose one.
.PP
A container that cannot be read is reported in the window rather than on the
standard error, because the argument usually arrives from a file manager where
nobody is watching a terminal.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns success when the window has been closed. A non\-zero status means the
window could not be created at all \(em no display, or no usable graphics
driver \(em rather than anything about a container.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/mime/packages/application\-x.slipcase+zip.xml
The media type, which is what makes a file manager offer this application for
a container. Matched by filename: the format reserves no magic bytes, so a
container is identified by its name.
.TP
.I /usr/share/applications/slipcase\-desktop.desktop
The desktop entry.
.SH NOTES
What the payload card says a payload would open with is whatever the desktop
answered, and where the desktop will not answer the card says nothing rather
than guessing. This application keeps no table mapping filenames to types.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR slipcase (1),
.BR xdg\-mime (1),
.BR update\-desktop\-database (1)
.PP
The container format is specified in SPEC.md in the slipcase repository, and
the design of this application in DESIGN.md in its own.
.SH AUTHOR
David M. Anderson
